WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.068
-Come on, pal, you look --

00:00:02.068 --> 00:00:04.036
Wow, you look gre--
You're all dressed up.

00:00:04.036 --> 00:00:07.607
I think you are the first guest
to dress up for me.

00:00:07.607 --> 00:00:09.175
-Dude, I always dress like this.

00:00:09.175 --> 00:00:11.044
-No, oh, my -- stop.
Really?

00:00:11.044 --> 00:00:12.345
Every day of your life?

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:14.047
-No, dude, I knew you were
coming back and thought

00:00:14.047 --> 00:00:15.148
you were all --
and I thought maybe

00:00:15.148 --> 00:00:16.449
we would do something special.

00:00:16.449 --> 00:00:18.218
So, I was like, "I'll get the
suit back on," you know?

00:00:18.218 --> 00:00:20.520
-You're a good man.
Thank you for doing that, buddy.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.321
Where -- Are you in London?

00:00:22.321 --> 00:00:24.123
-Yeah, no, we're in London.
And it's all good.

00:00:24.123 --> 00:00:27.193
And we're just sort of like --
Sorry for that.

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:29.562
Just a second.

00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:32.699
-Oh, my gosh!
Are you wearing shorts?

00:00:32.699 --> 00:00:34.033
You cheater!
-No, no!

00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:35.367
-You cheater!
-No.

00:00:35.367 --> 00:00:36.636
-Busted.
-No.

00:00:36.636 --> 00:00:39.705
-Busted.
-No. I'm just kidding.

00:00:39.705 --> 00:00:43.075
-Oh.
Hey, I know you just played --

00:00:43.075 --> 00:00:46.645
You do this thing every year,
"Soccer Aid."

00:00:46.645 --> 00:00:49.582
But this year you did, like,
an e-sports version of that.

00:00:49.582 --> 00:00:51.183
How did you get involved
in "Soccer Aid."

00:00:51.183 --> 00:00:53.620
Are you a good player?

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:57.323
-Ah, I'm a "good" player.
Yes.

00:00:57.323 --> 00:00:58.858
I'm a good person,
and I'm a player.

00:00:58.858 --> 00:01:00.660
-Yes.
You definitely are.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:03.963
-I get my backside
handed to me regularly.

00:01:03.963 --> 00:01:07.200
But I am a good person, and
I try and play for charities.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.569
And we've raised millions
and millions and millions

00:01:09.569 --> 00:01:12.639
and millions and millions
of bucks for UNICEF

00:01:12.639 --> 00:01:14.740
and children all over the world,
so it's been a really good --

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:16.642
It's been a really good ride
over the last 10 years.

00:01:16.642 --> 00:01:18.278
-But you also --
You do a lot of stuff.

00:01:18.278 --> 00:01:20.813
You also do a thing with,
uh, supporting

00:01:20.813 --> 00:01:25.351
Give and Go Athletics, which is
based in Philadelphia, correct?

00:01:25.351 --> 00:01:27.853
-Yeah. Give and Go Athletics
is something that we've gotten

00:01:27.853 --> 00:01:29.355
involved in just recently

00:01:29.355 --> 00:01:32.158
through a very good
connection of ours.

00:01:32.158 --> 00:01:34.694
And Philly's a --
I regard it as a second home,

00:01:34.694 --> 00:01:36.462
and I have a home there.

00:01:36.462 --> 00:01:38.197
We try to get there
as much as we can.

00:01:38.197 --> 00:01:41.500
I love the town.
Uh, Give and Go Athletics --

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.403
it's formed by two guys,
amazing guys, Caleb and Andre.

00:01:44.403 --> 00:01:46.305
They grew up on different sides

00:01:46.305 --> 00:01:48.975
of the gang line
in North Philly.

00:01:48.975 --> 00:01:50.677
They both became
collegiate athletes.

00:01:50.677 --> 00:01:52.912
They both became
behavioral health therapists.

00:01:52.912 --> 00:01:54.714
They both came back
to North Philly,

00:01:54.714 --> 00:01:58.884
met each other at the exact time
that the state took away

00:01:58.884 --> 00:02:01.620
all extracurricular
sporting activities

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.289
and after-school events
and stuff.

00:02:03.289 --> 00:02:06.559
So in step these two guys.
They're collegiate athletes.

00:02:06.559 --> 00:02:08.027
Like, incredible athletes,

00:02:08.027 --> 00:02:11.498
but they're also
behavioral health therapists.

00:02:11.498 --> 00:02:14.366
So what they started to do
is provide

00:02:14.366 --> 00:02:16.903
extracurricular sporting
activities and stuff like that.

00:02:16.903 --> 00:02:21.173
But at the same time, providing
sort of trauma-informed therapy.

00:02:21.173 --> 00:02:24.310
So, they've been doing it
for the last 11 years,

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:25.711
and they've been doing it
on a shoestring.

00:02:25.711 --> 00:02:27.579
And they've been doing it out of
the goodness of their own heart.

00:02:27.579 --> 00:02:30.583
And so we started to pair up
with them and all that.

00:02:30.583 --> 00:02:33.586
So, I mean, if you don't mind,
can I plug them?

00:02:33.586 --> 00:02:35.288
Can I give their website?
-Please do.

00:02:35.288 --> 00:02:36.922
That's why -- yeah.
We let everyone.

00:02:36.922 --> 00:02:39.625
-If you want to support a child
through the year,

00:02:39.625 --> 00:02:42.295
monthly donation is the best
thing that you can do.

00:02:42.295 --> 00:02:44.363
And what you want to do is,
send it to --

00:02:44.363 --> 00:02:45.531 line:0%
You want to go to

00:02:45.531 --> 00:02:48.901 line:0%
giveandgoathletics.org/
get-involved.

00:02:48.901 --> 00:02:52.938
That's giveandgoathletics.org/
get-involved.

00:02:52.938 --> 00:02:54.373
And get involved
with these guys,

00:02:54.373 --> 00:02:56.543
because they are fixing
the world.

00:02:56.543 --> 00:02:57.810
-I think it's so cool
that you do that.

00:02:57.810 --> 00:02:59.345
I love that.

00:02:59.345 --> 00:03:01.447
I was, you know, in quarantine.

00:03:01.447 --> 00:03:03.616
You spend a lot of time looking
at things on the Internet

00:03:03.616 --> 00:03:05.885
and getting creative
and all this stuff.

00:03:05.885 --> 00:03:09.989
But then I saw you in a thing
called "Star Force."

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:11.424
Let me see if I get it right.

00:03:11.424 --> 00:03:15.428
"Sci-Fisolation"?
Is that correct?

00:03:15.428 --> 00:03:17.730
-You're a true friend.
You're a true friend.

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:20.466
-I thought it was funny.
I love "Sci-Fisolation."

00:03:20.466 --> 00:03:22.135
Uh, I want to make sure
I got that.

00:03:22.135 --> 00:03:24.871
It's like a spoof
of "Star Trek" almost.

00:03:24.871 --> 00:03:27.039
It's like your own version
of "Star Trek."

00:03:27.039 --> 00:03:31.077
-It's a spoof homage.
It's like a "spomage."

00:03:31.077 --> 00:03:32.611
[ Laughter ]

00:03:32.611 --> 00:03:36.582
It basically is a kind of
spoof of "Star Trek."

00:03:36.582 --> 00:03:41.487
But we try to keep it as
sci-fisolated as possible.

00:03:41.487 --> 00:03:44.157
So, you know sometimes the guys
are coming up with

00:03:44.157 --> 00:03:46.559
really good ideas for like
a good-looking laser gun,

00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:48.294
and we're like,
"Yeah, that's cool.

00:03:48.294 --> 00:03:49.728
That's looks like
a really cool laser gun."

00:03:49.728 --> 00:03:51.097
And you're like,
"You know what?

00:03:51.097 --> 00:03:53.966
It was funnier when you were
using the remote control."

00:03:53.966 --> 00:03:55.434
-[ Laughs ]
Yes!

00:03:55.434 --> 00:03:59.538
-We had to pull ourselves back
from being too good.

00:03:59.538 --> 00:04:02.308
-I saw a lot of fans of yours
were online talking,

00:04:02.308 --> 00:04:04.843
because you were playing,
you know, obviously,

00:04:04.843 --> 00:04:09.415
there's a connection between you
and Patrick Stewart with "X-Men"

00:04:09.415 --> 00:04:12.251
and now with -- you're playing
kind of a, you know,

00:04:12.251 --> 00:04:14.420
captain of a ship,
kind of a "Star Trekky,"

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:15.922
they're like,
"Is there any world

00:04:15.922 --> 00:04:19.024
where you would play a young
Jean-Luc Picard?"

00:04:19.024 --> 00:04:22.428
-Jimmy, that is
the only world --

00:04:22.428 --> 00:04:24.163
that is the only world
that exists

00:04:24.163 --> 00:04:26.198
in which I will play
Jean-Luc Picard.

00:04:26.198 --> 00:04:28.735
Any other world
is just an alternate reality

00:04:28.735 --> 00:04:30.903
in a bad episode of "Star Trek."

00:04:30.903 --> 00:04:32.872
-Right.
So it's already --

00:04:32.872 --> 00:04:35.207
It's happened.
-It happened already?

00:04:35.207 --> 00:04:37.943
-If they don't hire me,
I'm gonna make it on my phone,

00:04:37.943 --> 00:04:40.880
which pinged.
Sorry about that.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:43.315
-[ Laughs ]
Yeah. No. Listen.

00:04:43.315 --> 00:04:44.784
I'm calling it right now.

00:04:44.784 --> 00:04:48.688
I'm doing the virtual lockdown
equivalent of rubbing my scent

00:04:48.688 --> 00:04:53.058
all over Patrick's face, and
saying this territory is mine.

00:04:53.058 --> 00:04:55.227
All the other bald actors
can [bleep] off.

00:04:55.227 --> 00:04:58.531
It's me.
[ Both laugh ]

00:04:58.531 --> 00:05:01.367
-Can we talk about
"The Sandman"?

00:05:01.367 --> 00:05:04.170
-Yes, please.
-This is an audible original.

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:07.673
It's a big deal.
A great cast.

00:05:07.673 --> 00:05:11.711
Did you record this
before lockdown?

00:05:11.711 --> 00:05:14.713
-No, I --
Weirdly, I was meant to do it

00:05:14.713 --> 00:05:16.015
in January or February

00:05:16.015 --> 00:05:17.750
when I was doing a play
in the West End.

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:21.086
And my voice was, like,
tired from the show.

00:05:21.086 --> 00:05:22.488
So I kept saying
to the director,

00:05:22.488 --> 00:05:24.890
"Do you mind if we push this
back until after the show?"

00:05:24.890 --> 00:05:26.058
And he said "Yeah, of course."

00:05:26.058 --> 00:05:28.527
So we pushed it back
until after the show.

00:05:28.527 --> 00:05:30.229
And it scheduled perfectly
with the arrival

00:05:30.229 --> 00:05:32.565
of COVID-19 in the U.K.

00:05:32.565 --> 00:05:35.368
So I wasn't able to record it.

00:05:35.368 --> 00:05:38.303
So around about April,
we built

00:05:38.303 --> 00:05:41.974
a kind of makeshift studio
in my spare bedroom.

00:05:41.974 --> 00:05:44.143
We just moved into a nice,
slightly bigger house,

00:05:44.143 --> 00:05:46.612
but we were
in a tiny flat before.

00:05:46.612 --> 00:05:48.981
And then,
our only spare bedroom,

00:05:48.981 --> 00:05:51.083
which was full of moving stuff,

00:05:51.083 --> 00:05:53.753
was just rammed
and full of this little, like,

00:05:53.753 --> 00:05:56.856
James-made recording studio.

00:05:56.856 --> 00:05:58.658
It took me a day-and-a-half
to build the thing.

00:05:58.658 --> 00:05:59.792
It took me a day and a half

00:05:59.792 --> 00:06:01.360
to figure out
how to work the thing.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:03.595
And then, it took about
a day-and-a-half to calibrate

00:06:03.595 --> 00:06:06.298
the computer and the baby face
and all that kind of stuff

00:06:06.298 --> 00:06:08.668
to make the recording work.
-Of course.

00:06:08.668 --> 00:06:11.437
-But weirdly, we recorded it in
like a day-and-a-half,

00:06:11.437 --> 00:06:13.939
because I was so late
to the table,

00:06:13.939 --> 00:06:15.874
all the other --
all the other, like --

00:06:15.874 --> 00:06:17.876
I think it's like a cast
of a hundred or something.

00:06:17.876 --> 00:06:19.711
-Yeah.
-They had all recorded

00:06:19.711 --> 00:06:22.681
their stuff, and I got to listen
to every single episode

00:06:22.681 --> 00:06:26.618
and then lay down my stuff,
so it was absolutely fantastic.

00:06:26.618 --> 00:06:27.953
-It's what we need know.

00:06:27.953 --> 00:06:32.057
It is, I think, over 11 hours
of audio, of material.

00:06:32.057 --> 00:06:33.859
And it's like,
that's what we need.

00:06:33.859 --> 00:06:36.362
We need podcasts,
we need shows, we need things.

00:06:36.362 --> 00:06:39.698
So I thank you for doing that,
and I want to let everyone know,

00:06:39.698 --> 00:06:42.802
"Sandman" is available
on Audible right now.

00:06:42.802 --> 00:06:45.604
-Thank you.
-James, I love you, bud.

00:06:45.604 --> 00:06:47.339
Thank you so much
for coming on our show.

00:06:47.339 --> 00:06:49.641
I want to see you
in person, pal.

00:06:49.641 --> 00:06:50.876
-Alright.
Maybe next time.

00:06:50.876 --> 00:06:52.078
Thank you so much, bud.
Thanks for being here.

